.TH REGNDB 4
.SH NAME
regndb, register \- the network database as machines join and leave it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ndb/regndb
[
.B -N
]
[
.BI -m " regmnt"
]
[
.BI -a " attr"
]
[
.BI -f " database"
] ...
.PP
.B ndb/register
[
.BI -m " regmnt"
]
[
.B -p
]
[
.B -r
]
.I svcname
[
.I "attr value"
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Ndb (6)
databases are text files: a machine is in the network database because
somebody wrote it there.
.IR Registry (4),
on the other hand, already knows how to let a service come and go \- an
entry lives exactly as long as the file descriptor that created it, and
vanishes when that descriptor is closed, when the registering process
dies, or when its connection to the registry drops.
What was missing was any way for a reader of the network database to see
that.
.PP
.I Regndb
is a file server that renders a registry as an
.IR attrdb (6)
database, which is the form
.IR cs (8),
.IR dial (2)
and everything above them read.
It serves 9P on its standard input, in the manner of
.IR registry (4),
and presents two files:
.TF index
.PD
.TP
.B ndb
The rendered database.
Its
.B Qid.vers
is incremented on every rendering, so that
.B Db.changed
of
.IR attrdb (2)
reports a change and
.B Db.reopen
picks one up.
.TP
.B ctl
Read for the registry being watched, the key attribute, the current
generation, the number of services rendered, the number skipped, the
number of attribute pairs dropped, and the last error.
A pair is dropped when its name or value has no
.IR attrdb (6)
spelling, and when a registration carries an attribute named
.BR regsvc ,
which
.I regndb
writes itself.
Write
.B refresh
to re-render immediately.
.PP
.I Regndb
watches the registry's
.B event
file, which blocks until the registry changes and coalesces changes, so
it costs nothing while the network is quiet.
It re-renders on each change and, when the registry becomes
unreachable, every
.B 2
seconds until it can attach again.
.PP
Each line of the registry's
.B index
is a service name followed by attribute/value pairs.
A service that carries the key attribute (\c
.BR sys ,
or the one named by
.BR -a )
becomes one entry of the database, keyed on that attribute; the service
name appears as
.BR regsvc .
A service that does not carry the key attribute is skipped: a registry
normally holds a mixture of registrations, and rendering, say, a Grid
service as a host entry would corrupt lookups.
So
.IP
.EX
ndb/register 'tcp!10.42.0.7!0' sys joiner1 ip 10.42.0.7 dom joiner1.test
.EE
.PP
becomes
.IP
.EX
sys=joiner1
	regsvc=tcp!10.42.0.7!0 dom=joiner1.test ip=10.42.0.7
.EE
.PP
Unless
.B -N
is given, the rendered file begins with a
.B database=
entry naming the static databases given by
.B -f
(default
.BR /lib/ndb/local ).
.B Db.open
of
.IR attrdb (2)
follows it, so opening the served
.B ndb
file yields the dynamic entries followed by the static ones, and
.I cs
needs no change.
.I Cs
opens its databases once, when it starts, so
.I regndb
must be mounted before it:
.IP
.EX
mount {ndb/registry} /mnt/registry
mount {ndb/regndb} /mnt/regndb
ndb/cs -f /mnt/regndb/ndb
.EE
.PP
.I Register
joins a registry.
It writes
.I svcname
and the given attribute/value pairs to the registry's
.B new
file and then holds that file descriptor open, so the registration lasts
exactly as long as the process: killing it, or losing the connection to
a remote registry, takes the machine out of the database.
With
.B -p
the entry is marked
.B persist
and
.I register
returns at once, leaving the entry until it is removed explicitly;
.B -r
removes the named entry.
.SH EXAMPLE
Serve one registry to the network, and let every machine on it be
looked up by name as it comes and goes:
.IP
.EX
mount {ndb/registry} /mnt/registry
listen -v 'tcp!*!registry' {export /mnt/registry&}
mount {ndb/regndb} /mnt/regndb
ndb/cs -f /mnt/regndb/ndb
.EE
.PP
On a machine that is joining, with the registry mounted from that
server:
.IP
.EX
ndb/register 'tcp!'^$ip^'!0' sys $sysname ip $ip &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ndb/regndb.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/ndb/register.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR attrdb (2),
.IR registry (4),
.IR ndb (6),
.IR cs (8)
.SH BUGS
.I Cs
opens its databases only at startup, so a
.I regndb
started afterwards is never seen; nothing reports that.
.PP
An attribute name or value containing a newline has no
.IR attrdb (6)
spelling; the pair is dropped and counted in
.BR ctl .
An empty value is written
.BR '' ,
which
.IR attrdb (2)
reads back as an attribute with no value.
.PP
A registry may hold two services carrying the same key value; the
rendered database then has two entries with that key, and a lookup finds
the first.
.PP
Re-rendering reads the registry's
.B index
synchronously in the process that also answers 9P, so a slow or distant
registry stalls readers of the served files.
